<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trinh Tung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1550">1550</a>–<a href="/years/1623">1623</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Trịnh Tùng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trinh Tung was the Trinh lord who ruled northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> behind the Later Le emperors from 1570 to 1623. A son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-kiem">Trinh Kiem</a>, he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thang Long</a> from the Mac dynasty in 1592 and executed the Mac emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-mau-hop">Mac Mau Hop</a>, restoring the Le court to the capital. He took the title of prince and made the Trinh regency hereditary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trinh-tung.jpg" alt="Trinh Tung" width="251" height="598" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trịnh Như Tấu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tr%E1%BB%8Bnh_T%C3%B9ng.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Trịnh Tùng, also called Trịnh Tòng, was born on 19 December 1550, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinh-kiem">Trịnh Kiểm</a>, who held the real power of Đại Việt as a duke behind the Lê emperors but never claimed the title of Trịnh lord. Tùng is therefore counted the first official Trịnh lord, although he was so young when his family came to power that in effect he belonged to its second generation. When his father died, command passed to his elder brother, Trịnh Cối, whom Tùng challenged at once; after Cối lost a battle to the Mạc, Tùng took charge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Lê loyalist army briefly took the Eastern Capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thang-long">Thăng Long</a>, from the Mạc emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mac-mau-hop">Mạc Mậu Hợp</a>, but was driven out by a counter-offensive. Amid the reverse the nominal Lê emperor, Lê Anh Tông, fled to Nghệ An; Tùng enthroned Lê Thế Tông in his place and had the former emperor killed. The war with the Mạc went on for twenty years, and further campaigns followed in 1593 and 1594 with help from the Nguyễn. In 1599 he was given the title Bình An Vương, Pacifying Prince. From his time the Trịnh lords chose the crown prince and controlled political and military affairs.</p><p>In 1619 Emperor Lê Kinh Tông plotted with Tùng's own son, Trịnh Xuân, to recover real power for the throne. The plot was discovered, the emperor was forced to garrotte himself and Lê Thần Tông was set up instead. Nguyễn Phúc Nguyên then formally broke off relations with the court, a break that after seven more years led to the Trịnh–Nguyễn War.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In old age Tùng tried to secure the succession by dividing power between two sons, but his family quarrelled before he died, a brother also reaching for power. Taken ill to his brother's house, he saw his younger son murdered there, while the elder, Trịnh Tráng, fled to Thanh Hóa with the emperor and the royal family. Driven from the house and abandoned by the servants carrying his sedan chair, he died alone on the road on 17 July 1623.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trịnh Tùng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war lasted until 1592, when <b>Trinh Tung</b> captured Thang Long and executed the Mac emperor Mac Mau Hop.</blockquote><a href="/state/mac-dynasty">Mac dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>After <b>Trinh Tung</b> took Thang Long in 1592 and executed Mac Mau Hop, members of the Mac dynasty fled to the mountainous province of Cao Bang near the Chinese border.</blockquote><a href="/state/mac-rule-in-cao-bang">Mac rule in Cao Bang</a></li><li><blockquote>His son <b>Trinh Tung</b> recaptured Thang Long from the Mac dynasty in 1592.</blockquote><a href="/state/trinh-lords">Trinh lords</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Thanh Oai, in what is now <a href="/countries/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=20.85,105.77&amp;year=1597" aria-label="Thanh Oai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Trinh Tung"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3874 5709h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3874 5709h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=20.85,105.77&amp;year=1597">What was here in 1597? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
